./.scratch/multi-catfish-v024-regime-b-design/REGIME-B-PHYSICS-BRIEF-GEMINI-2026-09-08.md:1:INDEPENDENT PHYSICS PROPOSAL (answer in Traditional Chinese with English technical terms; ≤ 1200 words; numbered; no code changes). A multi-user LEO handover simulator prices network energy efficiency EE = Σbits/Σjoules. Current energy model (from the code attached below): per-beam RF power = MAX over the beam's served users (not a sum); legal per-user power ≤ 1.65 W while PA saturation is 5.218 W (PA supply ≈ sqrt(p·p_sat)/0.35, monotone — no saturation penalty regime); PA ≈ 94.8 % of total power; each active beam adds a fixed circuit charge 0.338 W and each active satellite 0.200 W baseband; per-beam bandwidth 166.667 MHz shared equally by served users, so rate R_u = (B/n_b)·log2(1+SINR_u); no per-beam capacity/admission limit and no traffic-demand model; 100 users, 28 action slots per user, decisions every 30.08 s. In this regime, spreading load across beams costs joules faster than it earns bits, and consolidation loses bits faster than it saves joules; all coordination ("third Catfish", C3) attempts failed to raise pooled EE. The owner wants a SECOND regime, designed a priori and disclosed as such, in which coordination / load balance causally matters for EE. Propose: (1) the physically justified parameter or model changes (with literature anchors: real LEO PA efficiency curves and back-off, per-user power allocation / target-SINR power control, per-beam capacity or finite traffic demand, interference coupling, activation cost weights) that would create coordination headroom; for each: the mechanism in Δbits vs Δjoules, expected magnitude, side effects on the existing per-user surplus heads (C1: one-step bits − λ·energy; C2: persistence/service risk), and implementation risk; (2) a minimal set and a FIXED small grid (≤ 6 points) to declare before any run; (3) why raising the legal power cap alone would not help; (4) the single cheapest probe to confirm the regime has coordination headroom before retraining anything (an exact oracle ceiling over "at most one user changes" and over "evacuate one beam" already exists and runs in 17 minutes). Be concrete and quantitative where the attached code permits.
/home/sat/mcrl-hub-copy/dr9-prompts/PROMPT-9C-energy-model-realism.md:2:You are a satellite payload and link-budget specialist. The attached note gives the successor model's equations and a worked angle → power → EE table; the regime and synthetic reports give operating points. Model summary: per-user rate target r* = 50 Mbit/s; the lowest ACM mode from EN 302 307-1 Table 13 that meets the target under equal-airtime TDM with n_b users per beam; required RF power p_u = min(1.65 W, Γ_r·(N₀W + Î)/ĥ) solved as a coupled capped fixed point across beams; PA supply power √(p·p_sat)/0.35 with p_sat = 5.218 W evaluated per slot and averaged over airtime; 0.338 W per active beam chain; 0.200 W per active satellite; optional idle power 1/12 of PA supply at cap; bits and joules integrated over 47 sub-intervals of a 30.08 s step; service = SINR ≥ the mode threshold after joint resolution.
