./.scratch/multi-catfish-v023-controller-handoff-20260907/GLOBAL-VIEW-C3-QUESTION-2026-09-08.md:3:Context. A multi-user LEO handover RL system deploys a per-user decision `a_u* = masked argmax_a [Q1_u(a) + Q2_u(a) + z3_u(a)/κ]` (28 slots, 100 users, one central scheduler per 30.08 s interval). Q1 (228-D "C1") predicts each user's one-step handover surplus in bits minus λ·(network-energy delta); Q2 (448-D "C2", OPS-3) prices persistence/service risk over future offsets; the third head "C3" was meant to capture what one user's choice does to everyone else (interference, shared PA supply, beam activation/circuit/baseband charges, bandwidth sharing) — the "load balance / coordination" term. The scientific objective is pooled network energy efficiency EE = Σbits / Σjoules over matched TRAIN anchors with a served-fraction margin of 0.001 versus BASE = argmax(Q1+Q2). Over 09-01 → 09-07, eighteen attempts across eight C3 target families all failed to raise pooled EE with a deployable target; the striking facts are that even EXACT oracle labels composed through the additive per-user rule LOST EE (MONE −2.64%, 3/3 lineages; PNFE −11.1%; unilateral surpluses positive in 60/60 diagnostics while the realised joint direction reversed in 28), the only positive family (ZR +0.94–0.98%) depended on realised fading that no deployable policy sees (EXPECTED_ZR fast screen → STOP), the pairwise coalition test R7 gave −0.049% pooled (2/8 worlds positive), and the cost-share targets D/F gave −1.33% / −4.54% with service failing. The owner's question: "the low confidence must be about the current overall design, mainly the C3 target formula (r3); C1's EE formula is fixed and C2 has little influence — or are there other big influences? Load balance should be directly related to EE; how can it be impossible to find a way to improve EE? That seems unreasonable."
