     5	`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`. This record measures latency. It makes no learned-policy EE claim, uses no loss value as evidence about EE, performs no training, and changes nothing sealed.
     6	
     7	## Decision
     8	
     9	The current path does **not** fit reliably inside Contract v1 §F2's 10 s wall-clock budget. Presentation-to-commit latency exceeded 10 s on 13/20 anchors (**65% miss rate**): min / q25 / median / q75 / max / mean = **5.457 / 9.596 / 12.014 / 19.077 / 24.079 / 13.977 s**. Median overrun was 2.014 s and worst overrun was 14.079 s.
    10	
    11	Even if the post-argmax physical evaluation and bookkeeping are treated as part of the interval reserved for validation/commit rather than coordinator compute, the path through stable argmax was 5.271 / 9.323 / **11.532** / 17.128 / **22.018** / 13.033 s and missed on 11/20 anchors (55%). Catalogue construction alone exceeded 10 s on 9/20 anchors, so budget placement around the other stages cannot rescue those nine observations.
    12	
    13	Under [Contract v1 §F2](V025-STAGES-6-8-CONTRACT-v1-2026-09-08.md#L39), a miss is not a crash or an invalid run: the runner cancels the coordinator and executes the already validated `a⁰`; the miss is co-reported as QoS and its B/E remains in the endpoint. The instrumentation allowed late computations to finish solely so their latency could be measured. Thus the operational consequence on this observed panel would be fallback on missed anchors, not committing the late learned selection.
    14	
    15	## Verified by running code
    16	
    17	### Method and workload
    18	
    19	The panel is the same `FULL20` set used by the anytime receipts: `V025_PROBE_R2/world/{1..4}`, steps 0–4. The benchmark asserted all **20/20** nearest-eligible base assignments against `receipts/anytime-sealed-full20.json` before accepting an anchor. Eight-step tapes were needed to supply the deployed three-step feature forecast; tape loading was outside each anchor stopwatch (0.250–0.311 s per world in the successful run).
    20	
    21	The benchmark follows the deployed scalar path in [`run_v025_pilot_c3.py:1503–1578`](scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py#L1503): action deltas, contexts only for changed-set size at least two, additive-plus-interaction catalogue scores, and stable configuration-ID tie-breaking. It measures five arms: `FULL`, `DROP_C1`, `DROP_C2`, `DROP_C3`, and `ALL_NEUTRAL_CONTROL`.
    22	
    23	- Catalogue rows per anchor: min / q25 / median / q75 / max / mean = **965 / 977.5 / 991.5 / 999.25 / 1008 / 989**.
    24	- Catalogue score cells: **98,900 rows × arms** over the panel, or 4,825–5,040 per anchor.
    25	- The deployed action-head path does not forward every catalogue row independently. It computes reusable deltas from exactly 2,900 `(user, action)` rows per anchor. Because the production loop re-forwards candidate and reference through both Q1 and Q2, this is **58,000 scalar action-head forwards per anchor** across five arms, 1,160,000 over the panel.
    26	- Interaction forwards exist only for the 207–213 context-bearing rows per anchor: 1,035–1,065 per anchor across five arms, **21,055** over the panel.
    27	- The five arms collapsed to 2–4 unique selected configurations per anchor. The committed physical stage therefore executed 96–192 realised boundaries per anchor through the deployed batched evaluator.
