    78	
    79	### Coalition-context coverage
    80	
    81	There were 4,211 context-bearing rows and 15,569 context-free rows among 19,780 legal catalogue rows. The pooled `contexts.get(...) is None` fraction was **78.7108%**. Across anchors it was min / q25 / median / q75 / max / mean = **78.1347% / 78.5729% / 78.7769% / 78.9014% / 79.1751% / 78.7079%**. This is 0.4532 percentage points below the supplied C3REACH value of 79.164% and confirms the same sparse interaction-forward regime.
    82	
    83	### Head provenance and shape
    84	
    85	No training was run. Q1 and Q2 for all five arms came from the existing epoch-2000 checkpoints for learner seed `5166716249291843642`. Their shapes were Q1 `16→8→1` and Q2 `22→100→50→50→1`.
    86	
    87	The available epoch-2000 C3 checkpoints are `117→64→64→1`, but the current deployed `CoalitionContext.invariant_vector(member_width=38)` is 240-wide. Attempting the checkpoint produced a verified matrix-shape exception (`117 != 240`) before any anchor result was accepted. For the successful latency run, each arm therefore used a deterministic **untrained synthetic C3 head of the correct deployed shape `240→64→64→1`**, with the same scalar ReLU forward implementation. The assertion checked width 240 on every context-bearing anchor. This is shape-honest for the requested forward-pass cost; the learned values are irrelevant to that cost.
    88	
    89	The synthetic C3 values can affect which configurations the five arms select and hence how many distinct configurations the committed physical batch deduplicates. Accordingly, the physical-evaluation stage above is exactly measured for these synthetic-head selections, not attributed to an unavailable deployable trained C3 checkpoint.
    90	
    91	### Hardware, isolation, and memory
    92	
    93	- CPU: Intel Core Ultra 7 265KF, 20 logical CPUs, one thread per core; affinity CPUs 0–19.
    94	- Worker/process count: **one** Python process, `nice -n 15`; runtime ended with one native thread.
    95	- Interpreter: `/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover/.venv/bin/python` (Python 3.13.3); `PYTHONPATH=src`.
    96	- `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1`, `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`, `NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS=1`.
    97	- The machine had substantial other concurrent load, as required to be disclosed: load average was **15.80 / 15.58 / 14.79** at start and **14.71 / 15.48 / 14.98** at end. No other Python work or benchmark was run concurrently by this task.
    98	- Successful-run peak RSS: **1,490.422 MiB** (`/usr/bin/time` maximum RSS 1,526,192 KiB), below the 5 GB cap.
    99	
   100	Peak-RSS line printed by the benchmark:
   101	
   102	```text
   103	PEAK_RSS_MIB=1490.422
   104	```
   105	
   106	### Same-budget anytime exact comparator — reused, not recomputed
   107	
   108	The existing same-panel anytime record already supplies the required 10 s comparator, so no exact search was rerun. Under `SEALED`, at 10 s its pooled endpoint is **15.336646 Mbit/J** from 1.270120 Tbit / 82,816.015 J (served-count sum 1,597), a +311.74% pooled gain over its legal base. This is the incumbent a learned method must beat at the same budget, as the record itself states ([`ANYTIME-UNILATERAL-2026-09-10.md:9`](ANYTIME-UNILATERAL-2026-09-10.md#L9), [10 s row](ANYTIME-UNILATERAL-2026-09-10.md#L31)). The source receipt is `receipts/anytime-sealed-full20.json`, file SHA-256 `fc7f5a622ec3189fbd2656fd2a9795bdbe9ec072cce81b64834bb386c413a227`.
   109	
   110	For completeness only, the existing `MARGIN_Q` 10 s pooled endpoint is 25.155813 Mbit/J; it is a different provisioning rule and is not substituted for the `SEALED` comparator.
   111	
   112	## Derived on paper from the run receipt
   113	
   114	- Presentation-to-commit miss count: `13 / 20 = 65%`; hit count: 7/20.
   115	- Median budget margin: `10 - 12.014 = -2.014 s`; worst margin: `10 - 24.079 = -14.079 s`.
   116	- If the entire 1.5–2.5 s feature/action-row stage were generously removed from the clock—not merely the base computation that §F2 actually excludes—10/20 anchors would still miss and the median would still be **10.276 s**.
   117	- Catalogue construction alone exceeded 10 s on 9/20 anchors, establishing a **45% observed miss-rate floor** even if every other stage were free. This is a lower bound, not a new timed policy run.
   118	- With catalogue construction removed, all observed remaining work was 3.262–6.352 s per anchor (median 3.715 s). This identifies catalogue construction as the necessary first optimization target; it does not claim that removal is contract-admissible.
   119	
   120	## Inferred conclusion and required change
   121	
   122	The acceleration claim's latency prerequisite is **not live for the current deployed five-arm path on this loaded machine**. The learned forward passes are not the problem: all five arms' heads took 0.445 s median. `_catalogue_with_census` is the bottleneck: 8.299 s median, 19.184 s worst, and by itself exhausted the contract on nine anchors.
   123	
   124	To make the prerequisite live without changing §F2, catalogue generation—especially its exact nominal unilateral ranking/evaluation—must be made materially faster, then the full presentation-to-commit distribution must be rerun with the real deployable checkpoint and runner-enforced cancellation. The observed total needs at least **2.014 s** reduction at the median and **14.079 s** at the worst anchor; for all 20 observed anchors, catalogue time would have to fit the 3.648–6.738 s residual allowance left by their other work. Batching/vectorising or safely caching the repeated nominal ranking work is the evident engineering seam. Moving catalogue construction outside the timer would require an explicit timing-contract/provisioning amendment and equal treatment of the comparator; it cannot be assumed after seeing this result.
   125	
   126	Per §F2, until that work succeeds the valid operational behavior is: cancel at 10 s, execute validated `a⁰`, record a miss, and include fallback B/E in the endpoint. The current observation does **not** imply all arms always collapse to `a⁰`: seven five-arm anchor paths finished in time here. It does imply a material, reportable miss rate and no reliable fixed-point-quality-inside-10-s prerequisite yet.
   127	
   128	## Isolation caveats and reproducibility
   129	
   130	Base preparation could not be timed in isolation without replacing or rewriting the deployed `_build_anchor_rows` function: it rederives and boundary-zero evaluates the base inside feature construction. That whole stage remains conservatively charged above. The report therefore provides both presentation-to-commit and through-argmax totals rather than guessing a base-only subtraction.
   131	
   132	Model import/checkpoint restoration and world-tape construction/loading are per-process/per-world setup before an anchor is presented, so they are not amortized into anchor latency. The successful run's receipt and benchmark are:
   133	
   134	- `receipts/selector-latency-full20.json` — file SHA-256 `1f9ced246182820f0d8b8e54637a05333cc5a2bb3234f45b4de89b7a69cbabf9`; internal unsigned-payload receipt digest `abc4a489233ca2a82979b0f68d40102c23a94648b6506d375fdd3e18165e2c95`.
   135	- `harness/selector_latency.py` — SHA-256 `f782e40a8c236a9719894342f3a353c79efffb2b866b9cdf246daedb3afc44c8`.
