    65	- **STRICT 548→780 of 800:** `CONTROL-LAW-DEMAND-CAPPED-2026-09-10.md` reports exactly “Target attainment is 548 → 780 of 800 user-anchors.” Source: [demand-capped control-law report, line 46](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-floor-ws/CONTROL-LAW-DEMAND-CAPPED-2026-09-10.md:46). The detailed control-law receipt independently records 548 and 780. Source: [control-law receipt](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-arch-ws/.scratch/control-law-ceiling/control-law-ceiling.json).
    66	- **Spearman rho:** the 30-date report states `ρ=+0.006`; its aggregate receipt stores `spearman_rank_correlation = 0.006006674082313682`. Sources: [CEILING30 report, line 13](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/CEILING30-MARGIN-2026-09-10.md:13) and [aggregate receipt](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/.scratch/oracle-ceiling30-margin/ceiling30-margin-aggregate.json).
    67	
    68	These checks were read-only extractions and arithmetic over existing records. No simulation, measurement, model execution, or Python process was run, and no sealed material was modified.
    69	
    70	### The two prior attacks
    71	
    72	The record search found both attacks specified in the commission.
    73	
    74	1. On 2026-09-09 the controller recorded: “My rebuttal silently changed execution to `p_m`. That is a legitimate alternative controller, and its arithmetic is correct, but it answers a different question. I attacked a claim the declaration was not making.” Source: [first closed adjudication, lines 4–10](/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-ADJUDICATION-MARGIN-CLOSED-2026-09-09.md:4).
    75	2. On 2026-09-10 the controller recorded: “I rebuilt the same case against the same instruction, from a different direction, and did not notice,” then concluded that zero attainment was not a breached contract because no such promise was made. Source: [second adjudication, lines 6–15](/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-ADJUDICATION-PROVISIONING-NOT-YET-2026-09-10.md:6).
    76	
    77	## Derived on paper
    78	
    79	Let `Γ_target` be the threshold of the lowest mode meeting the nominal rate target, and let the tenth-percentile gain factor be `q < 1`. At an uncapped nominal fixed point, the declared power controller gives
    80	
    81	`SINR_nominal = Γ_target`.
    82	
    83	The v1.9 selection view therefore tests modes against
    84	
    85	`SINR_predicted = q · SINR_nominal = q · Γ_target < Γ_target`.
    86	
    87	Consequently the target mode cannot qualify in that view: the transmitted mode is a lower mode if one clears, otherwise no mode is transmitted. A lower mode can decode while its spectral efficiency remains below the `r*n/W` needed for the nominal per-user target. This is exactly the distinction between `m_target`, `m_tx`, decoded PHY service, and separately reported rate attainment that v1.1 and v1.8 require.
    88	
    89	This derivation explains why zero endpoint attainment and successful lower-rate decoding can coexist. It does not turn those receipt counts into a promise the declarations did not make.
    90	
    91	## Inferred
    92	
    93	The corrected STRICT convention is scientifically consequential. Its 548→780 attainment result and the near-zero rank correlation show that it is a different policy capable of changing both service outcomes and the ordering of per-date coordination gains. Those facts are reasons to label and preserve the two conventions distinctly; they are not evidence that the earlier convention violated its own declared semantics.
    94	
    95	Because this decision makes no model change, v1.8 §5's rerun requirement is not triggered. If a later decision chooses target-mode delivery at the design percentile as a new planning objective, that would reverse v1.9 §1 and would require a versioned amendment and reruns of every affected comparison. That hypothetical is not part of this closed item.
    96	
    97	## Strongest case for outcome (A), and answer
    98	
    99	The strongest case for (A) is not that higher power is automatically more physical or that STRICT helps a preferred EE result. It is this: a controller named for a 50 Mbit/s rate target provisions nominal SINR exactly at the target-mode threshold, then predictably de-rates that SINR before mode selection; all three endpoint censuses report zero target attainment, every one of 846,846 decoded original attempts uses insufficient spectral efficiency, STRICT restores substantial attainment, and the two policies' date-level coordination rankings are essentially unrelated. Calling the quantile a “decode reserve” and the mechanism a “rate-target power controller” can therefore appear to promise target delivery at the scoring percentile. On engineering usefulness and reviewer expectations, that is a serious argument for adopting a different planning objective.
   100	
   101	It does not establish the premise required for (A): a declaration promising an outcome the sealed rule fails to deliver. The text expressly says “A guaranteed-rate service endpoint is not declared,” calls 50 Mbit/s “nominal” and “synthetic,” calls the target a “power-control setpoint, not a demand model,” keeps actual partial delivery, separates PHY service from rate attainment, withdraws the availability rationale, and finally commands that the margin not be re-solved into power. Section 8's causality correction promises that the mode selected from the margin-adjusted nominal view is the mode actually transmitted and credited on successful decoding; it does not promise that `m_tx = m_target`. The adverse counts demonstrate the declared policy's consequences, not noncompliance with it.
   102	
   103	This reading survives the anti-straw-man check. It accepts the opposing side's strongest evidence—the zero attainments, the 846,846 insufficient-SE decodes, STRICT's restored attainment, the rank-order change, and v1.8's reserve language—and answers the actual contractual question. It does not replace that question with the weaker claim that the alternative is mathematically invalid or inconsequential. The alternative is coherent and consequential; it is simply a policy change, not a correction compelled by breach.
   104	
   105	## Closure
   106	
   107	**The sealed rule stands. The 0/2,000 observation is not a breach. PROVFIX is closed with no policy amendment and no rerun obligation.**

